  "verbalization": "Sodium Citrate is linked to Peptic Esophagitis through a series of indications that suggest a potential role in the pathogenesis of this condition. The evidence indicates that Sodium Citrate is used to treat Esophagitis, which in turn is indicated for Peptic Esophagitis. This creates a chain of therapeutic relationships that may imply a shared mechanism or pathway in the treatment of esophageal inflammation. The presence of multiple indications for Sodium Citrate—Camostat, Magnesium Trisilicate, and Silicon Dioxide—suggests that it may be used in combination with other agents to manage peptic esophagitis, possibly through different mechanisms of action. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the therapeutic relationships and the potential for Sodium Citrate to be part of a broader treatment strategy for this condition.",
